(9160, 76, 48, 'HONORARY AWARD', true, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'To Blake Edwards in recognition of his writing, directing and producing an extraordinary body of work for the screen.'),
(9161, 76, 47, 'GORDON E. SAWYER AWARD', true, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Peter D. Parks'),
(9162, 76, 60, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Academy Award of Merit)', true, NULL, 'Sound', 'The efficient algorithms, extensible architecture and intuitive interface have enabled Pro Tools to become the worldwide standard for the creation and editing of motion picture soundtracks.', 'To DIGIDESIGN for the design, development and implementation of the Pro Tools® digital audio workstation.'),
(9163, 76, 60, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Academy Award of Merit)', true, NULL, 'Systems', 'Measuring his valuable contributions to the invention and implementation of robotic camera systems in decades rather than years, his efforts have aided motion control in becoming a core technology that has supported the renaissance of visual effects.', 'To BILL TONDREAU of Kuper Controls for his significant advancements in the field of motion control technology for motion picture visual effects.'),
(9164, 76, 61, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Scientific and Engineering Award)', true, NULL, 'Projection', 'This high-speed studio projector produces an image quality equal to projectors with Geneva movements. With its unparalleled shuttle speed, reversibility and acceleration, this projector has set a new standard for post-production viewing as well as in traditional screening facilities.', 'To KINOTON GmbH for the engineering and development of the Kinoton FP 30/38 EC II Studio Projector.'),
(9165, 76, 61, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Scientific and Engineering Award)', true, NULL, 'Laboratory', 'This technology successfully controls the static charge buildup on processed intermediate and sound negative films during high speed printing operations.', 'To KENNETH L. TINGLER, CHARLES C. ANDERSON, DIANE E. KESTNER and BRIAN A. SCHELL of the Eastman Kodak Company for the successful development of a process-surviving antistatic layer technology for motion picture film.'),
(9166, 76, 61, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Scientific and Engineering Award)', true, NULL, 'Digital Imaging Technology', 'Through their respective pioneering efforts with AMS AudioFile, Waveframe and Fairlight, the work of these gentlemen contributed significantly to the development and realization of digital audio workstations with full editing capabilities for motion picture soundtracks.', 'To CHRISTOPHER ALFRED, ANDREW J. CANNON, MICHAEL C. CARLOS, MARK CRABTREE, CHUCK GRINDSTAFF and JOHN MELANSON for their significant contributions to the evolution of digital audio editing for motion picture post production.'),
(9167, 76, 61, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Scientific and Engineering Award)', true, NULL, 'Digital Imaging Technology', 'Massive takes a new approach in simulating behaviors of large numbers of computer-generated extras a.k.a. \"agents.\" Each \"agent\" contains a primitive software \"brain\" used to develop behavioral rules simulating a wide range of behaviors. In \"The Lord of the Rings\" trilogy, over 200,000 agents were controlled in several scenes.', 'To STEPHEN REGELOUS for the design and development of Massive, the autonomous agent animation system used for the battle sequences in \"The Lord of the Rings\" trilogy.'),
(9168, 76, 63, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Technical Achievement Award)', true, NULL, 'Photography', 'This versatile, modular and widely accepted cine viewfinder system is capable of properly displaying images in multiple formats ranging from 35mm anamorphic to super 16.', 'To KISH SADHVANI for the concept and optical design, PAUL DUCLOS for the practical realization and production engineering and CARL PERNICONE for the mechanical design and engineering of the portable cine viewfinder system known as the Ultimate Director''s Finder (UDF).'),
(9169, 76, 63, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Technical Achievement Award)', true, NULL, 'Digital Imaging Technology', 'This mathematical model contributed substantially to the development and implementation of practical techniques for simulating subsurface scattering of light in translucent materials for computer-generated images in motion pictures.', 'To HENRIK WANN JENSEN, STEPHEN R. MARSCHNER and PAT HANRAHAN for their pioneering research in simulating subsurface scattering of light in translucent materials as presented in their paper \"A Practical Model for Subsurface Light Transport.\"'),
(9170, 76, 63, 'SCIENTIFIC AND TECHNICAL AWARD (Technical Achievement Award)', true, NULL, 'Digital Imaging Technology', 'These groundbreaking techniques were used to create realistic-looking skin on digitally created characters.', 'To CHRISTOPHE HERY, KEN McGAUGH and JOE LETTERI for their groundbreaking implementations of practical methods for rendering skin and other translucent materials using subsurface scattering techniques.'),
(9171, 76, 51, 'JOHN A. BONNER MEDAL OF COMMENDATION', true, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'To Douglas Greenfield in appreciation for outstanding service and dedication in upholding the high standards of the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences.'),
